1. Multifunction Devices (MFDs)
  
  Multifunction devices combine printing, scanning, copying, and faxing (less common today, but still present on many models) into a 
  single unit — consolidating hardware and saving space in offices and homes. Many support wireless/network printing, and some support 
  cloud printing and remote management depending on make and model.
  
  Installation process: unbox and remove shipping tape/adhesive, connect power and configure initial device settings, install drivers 
  and software on the computer first (so the device is auto-detected once connected), connect via USB or configure networking, then 
  perform a test print to verify functionality.
  
  Print settings to configure: default paper size (letter, legal, envelope, etc.), print quality/resolution measured in DPI 
  (higher DPI = better quality but more ink/toner use), duplex printing (both sides of the paper), and default tray/media type 
  (standard vs. photographic paper, etc.).
  
  Scan settings to configure: scan resolution, output document format (PDF, JPEG, TIFF), destination (scan-to-email, network folder, or 
  cloud storage), and color/contrast adjustments (higher resolution scans produce larger files but don't affect ink/toner).
  
  Wired connectivity: USB (single local device) or Ethernet (shared network device) — for Ethernet, assign a static IP so it doesn't 
  change, and confirm DHCP won't hand that address to another device.
  
  Wireless connectivity: Wi-Fi Direct for direct peer-to-peer connections; WPS (Wi-Fi Protected Setup, often a push-button pairing) or
  manual SSID entry to join a home/office network; optional mobile printing standards like AirPrint (Apple), Google Cloud Print, or 
  Mopria (Android).
  
  Troubleshooting: can't print at all → check/update drivers, verify physical or Wi-Fi connectivity, clear and resubmit the print 
  queue, restart the printer or the print spooler service. Scanning/copying issues → verify drivers, check for paper jams (especially 
  in the ADF), adjust scan settings if images are blurry. Network issues → restart the router or device, verify IP configuration, and 
  check whether a firewall is blocking print traffic.

2. Device Drivers
  
  Drivers are software packages that let the operating system communicate with hardware — printers, graphics cards, network adapters, and 
  virtually anything installed into a system.
  
  Kernel-mode drivers are pre-installed for core internal components (memory, processor, storage) — users generally don't interact with 
  these directly. User-mode drivers are for peripherals users add themselves (sound cards, network cards, printers) and can be installed, 
  accessed, and configured by the user.
  
  Using the correct driver for the exact OS version and device model matters for stability, performance, and full feature 
  availability — a driver built for a different OS version or device generation may still install and partially work, but without 
  exposing every feature. Manufacturer-supplied drivers are generally the safest choice; third-party drivers found online may work but 
  less reliably.
  
  Installation methods: automatic updates (through the OS itself, common when the manufacturer partners with the OS vendor), manual 
  installation (running the manufacturer's installer, historically via disc, now more often downloaded), or driver discs (still a manual 
  install). Even after manual installation, the vendor's own software may separately check for updates on its own schedule.
  
  Best practices: back up the current driver before updating, use only official manufacturer or OS-vendor sources to avoid malicious 
  software, and keep BIOS/firmware updated for full driver compatibility.
  
  Troubleshooting: device not recognized → likely an incorrect driver (wrong OS, wrong model) or a bad third-party driver, or a physical 
  connectivity problem. Performance/feature problems → often an OS mismatch or driver misconfiguration; roll back to the previous 
  driver if needed. Driver conflicts → sometimes the old driver must be fully removed before the new one installs.
  
  Driver management best practices: perform regular updates, use manufacturer tools (like Device Manager in Windows, or vendor utilities 
  from Intel, NVIDIA, HP, etc.) for full configuration options, avoid unknown third-party installers, and create system restore points 
  before major driver updates — this lets you revert everything at once, versus a single driver rollback through Device Manager for 
  one specific update.

3. Device Connections
  
  Device connectivity comes down to wired and wireless options, and for printers specifically, the mix determines how it's shared.
  
  Wired options: USB (local connection to one computer), Ethernet (the printer gets its own IP address directly on the network), and 
  HDMI/DisplayPort (used almost exclusively for external monitors or similar multimedia devices). A printer can also be installed 
  locally via USB on a print server and then shared — network users see it as a "network printer" even though there's no visible 
  Ethernet cable on the printer itself.
  
  Wireless options: Bluetooth (typically simpler peripherals like keyboards, mice, mobile devices — printers usually don't use this), Wi-Fi 
  (the printer connects directly to the network and gets its own IP address, no print server needed), and NFC (only for very short, 
  momentary connections like mobile-to-mobile file transfers — never used for a semi-permanent device connection like a printer).
  
  Printer connectivity types recap: USB = always a local install on one computer (though that computer can still share it over the 
  network). Direct network printer (wired or wireless) = the device gets its own IP address, no dedicated print server required, common 
  in small offices. Cloud printing = the printer doesn't live in the cloud, but both the user and the printer connect to a cloud 
  service, letting users print to it from anywhere with internet access.
  
  Troubleshooting: device not recognized → check cable or wireless connectivity, update drivers. Connection suddenly stopped → check 
  cabling/wireless first (less likely a driver issue if it worked before). Intermittent connectivity → confirm Bluetooth/Wi-Fi are 
  enabled, check for interference (other wireless devices, baby monitors, cordless phones, microwaves). Slow transfers → use USB 3.0 or 
  higher for wired connections, or check Wi-Fi range and obstructions for wireless.

4. Publicly Shared Devices
  
  Shared devices (most commonly printers, but also scanners and network storage) are accessible to multiple users on a network — reducing 
  hardware costs, improving efficiency, and centralizing management and collaboration.
  
  Direct printer sharing: the printer connects straight to the network (e.g., via Wi-Fi) and anyone on that network can print to 
  it — simple, but with minimal control over who can print.
  
  Network printer sharing (via a print server): the printer is installed locally on a dedicated server (often via USB) and then 
  shared, giving administrators far more control over access.
  
  Cloud-based printing: both the printer and remote users connect to the same cloud service, letting traveling users submit jobs 
  that print at a fixed office location.
  
  Print servers provide queue management for multiple print requests, user authentication and access control (who can print, 
  and when), and monitoring/logging of usage (who printed what, when, and from where) — useful for restricting confidential print
  jobs or dedicating certain printers to large jobs.
  
  Best practices for print server deployment: assign a static IP to the print server, use enterprise-grade security protocols to 
  protect data in transit, and keep firmware/drivers updated.
  
  Security considerations: unauthorized access can lead to print job interception or data leakage — both digitally (intercepted jobs) and 
  physically (anyone can walk up and grab a printed confidential document). Mitigations include enabling user authentication for printer
  access, using encryption for network print jobs, restricting access via role-based access control (RBAC) or explicit user/group
  permissions, and enabling audit logging (which is not on by default for shared printers, even in a network environment with its own 
  login logging).
  
  Troubleshooting: printer/device not found → standard network troubleshooting (verify connection, IP address, restart the print 
  spooler service). Slow printing → check for network congestion or a backed-up print queue, and check driver/firmware versions. 
  Permissions/access-denied errors → not a technical fault, just a configuration issue — contact administration to correct access rights.

5. Device Configuration Settings
  
  Printer configuration settings ensure optimal performance, let jobs be customized to specific needs, and reduce paper/ink waste — though 
  most printers work fine on default settings for routine use.
  
  Duplex printing: printing on both sides of the paper — mechanically, the printer prints one side, flips the sheet, then prints the
  other (not literally simultaneous).
  
  Page orientation: portrait (taller than wide, most common for text documents) vs. landscape (wider than tall, more common for 
  images) — not every printer supports both equally well, but virtually all support setting orientation.
  
  Feed tray and paper type: select the correct tray for the paper size in use (letter 8.5x11, legal 8.5x14, various envelope sizes) and
  paper type (standard, photographic/glossy, cardstock). Many trays have adjustable guides for different sizes.
  
  Print quality modes: draft (lowest quality, saves ink/toner, good for work-in-progress), standard (typical default, fine for most
  documents), and high quality (best results, but uses the most ink and takes the longest). These modes are really presets for the
  underlying DPI (dots per inch) setting — higher DPI means sharper but slower prints; lower DPI means faster but less detailed prints. 
  Additional ink-saving options include grayscale (black-and-white) printing for non-color documents and eco mode (still color, but 
  reduced ink consumption and vibrancy).
  
  Troubleshooting: light/faded printouts → check for eco mode or an unintentionally lowered DPI setting, or low ink/toner. Frequent
  paper jams → check the pickup rollers for misalignment or debris buildup, and clean as needed. Misaligned printouts (info off-position, 
  printing outside margins) → check printhead alignment (often has a built-in maintenance routine) or the application's own margin settings.

6. Device Security
  
  Printer/device security prevents unauthorized access to sensitive printed data, protects against cyber threats and physical 
  tampering, and helps maintain compliance with data protection regulations. Available security features vary heavily by make, model, and 
  the security requirements of the environment.
  
  Common threats: unauthorized print jobs leading to data leaks, network vulnerabilities enabling remote access exploitation, and lack
  of user authentication increasing misuse risk. A key physical risk: even a securely transmitted confidential document can be picked
  up by anyone standing at the printer tray.
  
  Authentication methods: username/password, smart card/badge authentication (tap to a reader), or biometric authentication 
  (fingerprint) — required before a job can even be submitted.
  
  Access control strategies: role-based access control (RBAC) restricts privileges by assigning users to roles with predefined 
  permissions; network authentication ensures only authorized users/devices can even connect to the network in the first place 
  (similar to logging into a Wi-Fi network).
  
  Securing the printed output itself: requiring authentication before printing, secure spooler settings that delete jobs immediately 
  after printing (rather than leaving them available for resubmission), PIN-based release (must enter a PIN at the device to release
  the physical printout), badge-based release, and encrypting print job traffic in transit.
  
  Audit logs and monitoring: track device activity for security/compliance, generate reports, identify unauthorized access or suspicious 
  activity, and configure real-time alerts for unusual print behavior. Logs can track submitted jobs, who accessed the logs themselves, and 
  device error logs (useful for troubleshooting hardware or configuration problems).
  
  Best practices: enforce strong, regularly changed passwords and valid credentials at the network level; physically secure the device
  (locked room, badge access, security cameras) especially for confidential printing environments; use encrypted protocols for print traffic.

7. Network Scanning Services
  
  Network scanning services let a centralized, network-attached scanner send scanned documents directly to email, network folders
  (SMB), or cloud storage — without needing a local computer at the scanner itself. This improves document workflow, reduces paper
  usage, increases accessibility and security, and supports centralized document management.
  
  Scan-to-email: the scanner sends the scanned document as an email attachment directly, using a configured mailbox (typically a 
  shared/departmental address like info@company.com rather than an individual's). Requires correct SMTP settings (mailbox, address, 
  password); firewall/port settings may need adjustment if mail isn't sending; scan resolution may need lowering if attachments are too large.
  
  Scan-to-SMB: sends the scanned document to a shared network folder using the Server Message Block protocol (common in Windows, also 
  supported on Linux/Unix) — useful for centralized file management where many people need access. Setup involves assigning the scanner a
  static IP/hostname, configuring the SMB protocol and destination folder path, and ensuring correct permissions via a service account
  that has write access to that folder.
  
  Cloud scanning: sends scans directly to cloud storage platforms (Google Drive, Dropbox, OneDrive, etc.) — conceptually similar to SMB 
  scanning but the destination lives in the cloud, letting users access documents from anywhere with internet access. Requires the 
  scanner to support cloud authentication (API/interface) and configuration of a destination folder and, optionally, automatic file 
  format conversion.
  
  Troubleshooting: authentication failures (any of the three methods) → verify credentials/passwords in the scanner's settings. File
  transfer failures → check network connectivity, firewall settings, and destination write permissions. Slow scanning → reduce resolution
  or output file size, check Wi-Fi connectivity (or prefer a wired connection where reliability matters).

8. Automatic Document Feeders (ADFs)
  
  An ADF allows multiple pages to be scanned, copied, or faxed without manual page-by-page loading — either built into the device or
  added as an aftermarket attachment. This increases efficiency, reduces manual handling and document wear, and supports batch loading
  for large jobs.
  
  Types of ADFs:
    
    - Simplex — loads and processes one page, one side, at a time.
    - Duplex — scans both sides of a page in a single pass (most efficient for large jobs).
    - Reversing — scans one side, flips the page mechanically, then scans the other side; cheaper than full duplex but slower.
    - Continuous — designed for very high-volume operations with minimal manual intervention, typically found in large enterprise devices.
    
  Configuration and use: ensure proper alignment and secure attachment (for aftermarket units), load documents face-up or face-down per
  manufacturer guidance, adjust paper guides to prevent misfeeds, remove staples/paper clips before loading, and use higher-quality paper
  to reduce jams.
  
  Troubleshooting: paper jams → check for torn/wrinkled pages, open the cover and clear stuck sheets. Multiple pages feeding at
  once → ensure proper separation (no staples, fan the stack to reduce static cling). Blurry/incomplete results → clean the scanner
  glass and ADF rollers, check resolution settings. ADF not recognized → check firmware updates, restart the device, verify all 
  connections (especially for aftermarket modules).
  
  Maintenance and optimization: clean rollers and scanner glass periodically with a lint-free cloth, monitor roller wear and replace
  as needed, keep firmware/drivers updated (both the device and any aftermarket ADF module), use predefined scanning profiles for
  different document types, enable real-time error detection for jams, and train users on proper loading technique.

9. Laser Printers
  
  Laser printers use laser technology and toner (a fine powder, not liquid ink) to produce high-resolution output at fast 
  speeds — historically an office standard, now also common at home as prices have dropped. They tend to cost more upfront than 
  inkjets but are typically cheaper per page for high-volume printing, since toner generally lasts longer than ink cartridges.
  
  The 7-step laser printing process: (1) data processing — the printer receives and processes the print job; (2) the photosensitive 
  drum receives a positive electrostatic charge; (3) the laser writes the image by discharging specific areas of the drum; (4) 
  developing — toner is attracted to the discharged areas of the drum; (5) transferring — the toner is applied to the paper; (6) 
  fusing — heat and pressure permanently bond the toner to the paper (this is why printed pages come out warm).
  
  Why choose laser over inkjet: faster print speeds, lower cost per page at high volume, and longer-lasting consumables (toner can last
  months in high-volume use versus weeks for ink cartridges at similar volume).
  
  Key components: toner cartridge (replaceable), photosensitive drum (transfers toner to paper), fuser assembly (applies heat/pressure), 
  laser assembly (directs the beam for image formation), and transfer belt/roller (moves paper through the process). Most components are 
  modular and replaceable, though for lower-end home units it's often cheaper to replace the whole printer than to service individual 
  parts (aside from the toner cartridge, which is always meant to be replaced).
  
  Maintenance: clean components regularly to prevent dust buildup, replace toner before it's fully depleted (running it to empty causes
  excess wear on the fusing/transfer process), check for firmware updates, and calibrate regularly for accurate color reproduction.
  
  Troubleshooting: streaks/smudges → clean components (rollers, toner transfer path). Faint/uneven prints → toner running low, replace it. 
  Paper jams → calibrate the printer and clean/inspect pickup rollers.
  
  Extending printer life: use high-quality toner and paper, store the unit in a dust-free, well-ventilated area (laser printers run warm),
  and keep the fuser unit clean for even heat distribution.

10. Inkjet Printers
  
  Inkjet printers use liquid ink sprayed through small nozzles — well suited to home/office use and especially strong for color quality 
  and photo printing. Lower upfront cost than laser printers, but typically higher cost per page over time, and a more compact design overall.
  
  Two ink-delivery technologies:
    
    - Thermal Bubble Jet: heats ink to create a vapor bubble that pushes it onto the paper — lower cost, simpler design, generally faster
      print speeds.
    - Piezoelectric: uses an electric current to precisely control ink release — higher precision, more versatility, and supports a wider 
      variety of ink types.
  
  Key components: ink cartridges (typically one black cartridge plus three color cartridges that mix to produce other colors), print
  head (deposits ink onto the paper as it moves back and forth), feed mechanism (moves paper through the printer), and a cleaning
  mechanism to prevent clogs.
  
  Maintenance: run print head cleanings regularly (via software or the device's own menu), store unused ink cartridges in their original
  sealed packaging to prevent drying out, and use high-quality paper to reduce smudging.
  
  Troubleshooting: streaky/faded prints → run a print head cleaning or alignment. Frequent paper jams → verify correct paper size/tray
  and tray guide adjustment. Ink not recognized → reseat the cartridge fully.
  
  Extending printer life: keep firmware updated, use genuine (manufacturer) ink cartridges for best performance, keep the device 
  clean, print non-essential documents in draft mode, turn the printer off when not in use to help prevent nozzle clogs, and avoid 
  leaving depleted cartridges installed for long periods.

11. Thermal Printers
  
  Thermal printers use heat-sensitive paper and thermal print heads rather than ink or toner — common in point-of-sale systems, shipping
  labels, and medical devices, but rarely found as standard home/office printers. They print fast and quietly with lower maintenance needs
  than ink-based printers.
  
  Direct thermal printing: heat directly darkens heat-sensitive paper to create the image — used for receipts and shipping labels, but
  these fade significantly over time (a year-old receipt is often unreadable).
  
  Thermal transfer printing: uses a ribbon that transfers wax or resin onto the paper via heat, producing a much more durable, 
  long-lasting result than direct thermal.
  
  Key components: print head (generates heat to transfer the image), platen roller (moves paper smoothly through the printer), ribbon 
  (thermal transfer models only), and sensors (detect paper position for proper feed).
  
  Maintenance: clean the print head with isopropyl alcohol to prevent buildup, check and replace worn rollers, and store paper/ribbon away 
  from dust and excessive heat.
  
  Troubleshooting: faded prints → clean the print head or increase heat settings if adjustable. Paper jams → check rollers and confirm
  correct paper type is being used. Blurry/smudged text → adjust print speed and check ribbon alignment (ribbon-based models).
  
  Extending lifespan: store paper/ribbon in a dry, cool environment away from heat sources, use high-quality thermal paper for 
  longevity, and regularly inspect cables/connections.

12. Impact Printers
  
  Impact printers use mechanical force to press an inked ribbon onto paper — functioning much like a typewriter. Now less common 
  overall, but still used in point-of-sale systems, invoice printing, and industrial applications.
  
  Types:
    
    - Dot matrix — uses pins striking the ribbon to form characters (or images) out of tiny dots; the most flexible impact type since 
      it can print anything assembled from dots.
    - Daisy wheel — a rotating wheel with pre-formed characters (letters, numbers); can only print those fixed characters, not images.
    - Line printer — prints entire lines at once, used in high-volume environments.
    
  Why still choose impact printing: it's the only printer type that can produce true multi-part carbon copies in a single pass
  (useful for customer/internal copy pairs), it's more reliable in harsh environments where other printer types may fail, and it 
  has a lower long-term operational cost per page.
  
  Key components: print head (contains pins or hammers), ink ribbon cartridge (the ribbon itself can typically be cycled/refreshed), tractor
  feed (uses perforated side strips with pin-wheels to keep paper precisely aligned during impact, with tear-off perforated edges), and 
  a platen roller (provides solid backing behind the point of impact).
  
  Maintenance: clean the print head to prevent ink buildup, replace ribbons before they dry out, and keep the paper feed mechanism clear
  of debris.
  
  Troubleshooting: faded prints → replace the ribbon or adjust print head alignment. Paper jams → check tractor feed alignment. Misaligned
  text → adjust print head position and/or platen roller tension.
  
  Extending lifespan: store ribbons in a cool, dry environment, lubricate mechanical components per manufacturer guidance (using only
  specified lubricants), avoid excessive force on the tractor feed, and monitor print jobs to catch errors before they cause hardware damage.

13. 3D Printers
  
  3D printers use additive manufacturing to build objects layer by layer — common in prototyping, manufacturing, and custom fabrication, 
  working with materials like PLA (polylactic acid), ABS (acrylonitrile butadiene styrene), and resin.
  
  Three primary technologies:
    
    - FDM (Fused Deposition Modeling) — melts and extrudes plastic filament layer by layer; the most common and affordable method.
    - SLA (Stereolithography) — uses UV light to harden liquid resin into solid objects; produces higher-resolution, finer-detail results.
    - SLS (Selective Laser Sintering) — uses a laser to fuse powdered materials (nylon, even metal); common for industrial/high-strength 
      applications.
  
  Key components: extruder/hot end (FDM — heats and deposits filament), build platform (supports the model during printing), stepper motors
  (control print head and platform movement), and a resin tank (SLA — holds liquid resin for curing).
  
  Maintenance: regularly clean the nozzle and build plate, check filament/resin levels before printing, and keep moving parts lubricated.
  
  Troubleshooting: warping → use a heated bed or adhesive to improve bed adhesion. Under-extrusion → check the filament feed and clean
  the nozzle. Layer shifting → check belt tension and stepper motor function.
  
  Extending lifespan/optimizing performance: store filament in a dry, sealed container to prevent moisture absorption, keep firmware 
  updated, maintain calibration settings, use correct print speed/temperature for each material, place the printer on a stable, vibration-free
  surface, and test settings on small models before large print jobs.

14. Printer Maintenance (General)
  
  Regular maintenance across any printer type prevents hardware failures, ensures consistent print quality, extends lifespan, reduces
  downtime, and improves efficiency (fewer jams, less wasted ink/toner).
  
  Common maintenance tasks: regularly clean print heads and rollers (often via an automated cleaning cycle in the device's own
  settings, occasionally supplemented by manual cleaning with a lint-free cloth and isopropyl alcohol), keep firmware and drivers
  updated, and properly store/handle ink and toner cartridges.
  
  Driver/firmware updates: improve OS compatibility, fix bugs, and patch security vulnerabilities. Driver updates sometimes arrive
  via standard OS updates (less common for printers than simpler devices, but possible), while firmware updates almost always come 
  through the printer's own dedicated software — check that automatic update checking is enabled if prompts aren't appearing.
  
  Cartridge handling best practices: store ink/toner in a cool, dry place, keep cartridges in their original sealed packaging until
  needed, gently shake new toner cartridges before installing (to prevent clumping), ensure proper alignment when inserting cartridges, print
  a test page after installation, avoid touching nozzles/print heads, and never use expired ink or toner.
  
  Common issues: streaks/blurry prints → clean the print heads, use higher-quality ink and paper. Paper not feeding properly → check and
  clean (or replace, if possible) the pickup rollers. Low ink warnings → these typically appear well before the cartridge is empty
  (e.g., at 20% or 10% remaining); replace once quality visibly declines. Slow printing → lower the print quality setting (e.g., draft mode) 
  for non-essential jobs.
  
  Preventative maintenance: run maintenance tasks on a regular schedule based on usage volume (more use = more frequent maintenance), use
  genuine ink/toner over generic alternatives for reliable performance, and regularly inspect internal components.

15. Setting Up Wireless Printing
  
  General installation flow for a local (USB or Wi-Fi) printer or multifunction device:
    
   1. Download the driver/software package directly from the manufacturer's support site, selecting the correct operating system version.
   2. Run the installer before physically connecting the device — for USB connections especially, don't plug in the cable until the installer
      prompts you to.
   3. Accept the license and choose which software components to install (drivers, utilities, any bundled apps).
   4. At the connection step, choose wireless or direct USB connection.
   5. For wireless setup: either configure the Wi-Fi connection (SSID and password) directly through the device's own menu beforehand, or 
      configure it during the installer's setup process — both achieve the same result. If the printer is already on the network, the installer
      can simply search for and detect it.
   6. Approve any firewall prompts needed for the installer's network components to function.
   7. Print a test page to confirm everything is working, and install any available firmware update offered at the end of setup.
    
  This same general flow — install software first, then connect (USB) or configure Wi-Fi (wireless), then test print — applies to virtually
  any modern local printer or multifunction device, regardless of manufacturer.

Quick Exam-Focus Reminders
  
  - Printer connection types: USB = local single-computer install; wired/wireless network printer = its own IP address, no dedicated server
    needed; print server = shared local install with centralized control; cloud printing = remote job submission to a fixed physical printer.
  - ADF types, in order of capability: simplex (one side) → reversing (two sides, flips the page) → duplex (two sides in one 
    pass) → continuous (very high volume).
  - Laser printing steps: data processing → drum charges → laser writes/discharges → developing (toner adheres) → transferring (toner
    to paper) → fusing (heat/pressure bonds toner).
  - Printer type comparison: laser = high volume, low cost/page, toner-based, fast; inkjet = better color/photo quality, lower upfront
    cost, higher cost/page; thermal = no ink/toner, heat-sensitive paper or ribbon, POS/labels; impact = mechanical force, only type that
    produces true carbon copies; 3D = additive manufacturing (FDM/SLA/SLS).
  - Kernel-mode drivers = pre-installed, core system components, not user-configurable; user-mode drivers = peripherals users install and
    manage themselves.
  - Print server functions: queue management, authentication/access control, usage monitoring and logging (logging is not on by default).
  - Printer security layers: authentication to submit a job (password/badge/biometric) + secure release (PIN/badge at the device) + encrypted
    transmission + audit logging.
  - Scan destinations: scan-to-email (SMTP, needs a mailbox), Scan-to-SMB (network folder, needs a service account with write permission),
    scan-to-cloud (needs the scanner to support cloud auth/API).
  - DPI controls both quality and speed — higher DPI means sharper output but slower, larger print jobs; draft/standard/high-quality modes
    are just DPI presets.
